<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marian Anderson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Marian Elina-Blanche Anderson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marian Anderson was an American contralto from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> who sang music from opera to spirituals with leading orchestras in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between 1925 and 1965. In 1939 the Daughters of the American Revolution refused to let her sing to an integrated audience in Constitution Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>; with the help of Eleanor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> she sang instead on the steps of the Lincoln Memorial on Easter Sunday, before more than 75,000 people. In 1955 she became the first Black American singer to perform at the Metropolitan Opera. She later served as a delegate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> Human Rights Committee, sang at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington">March on Washington</a> in 1963 and died in 1993.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marian-anderson.jpg" alt="Marian Anderson" width="330" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: S. Hurok-classical concert promoter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marian_Anderson_1951.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Marian Anderson</b> sang it in 1953 to a television audience of 60 million.</blockquote><a href="/songs/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Marian Anderson</b> recorded it for Victor in November 1923 and Paul Robeson on 10 May 1927; in the film Show Boat (1929) Laura La Plante mouthed it to the singing of Eva Olivetti, and Robeson sang it with a male chorus in The Proud Valley (1940).</blockquote><a href="/songs/deep-river">Deep River</a></li><li><blockquote>On Easter Sunday, 9 April 1939, the African American contralto <b>Marian Anderson</b> sang it at the Lincoln Memorial: she had been barred from singing at the DAR Constitution Hall under its whites-only policy, and after a national outcry, and with the support of Eleanor Roosevelt, the concert was…</blockquote><a href="/songs/my-country-tis-of-thee">My Country, 'Tis of Thee</a></li><li><blockquote>The contralto <b>Marian Anderson</b> had her first successful recording with the song, on the Victor label, in 1925.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nobody-knows-the-trouble-ive-seen">Nobody Knows the Trouble I've Seen</a></li></ul></section>
